<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kapellmeister">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kapellmeister</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kapellmeister is a German title for the director of music at a princely court or church, originally the leader of a chapel's musicians. In the baroque period a Kapellmeister composed, rehearsed and directed music for services, ceremonies and entertainments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> held the post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> from 1717 to 1723, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-philipp-telemann">Georg Philipp Telemann</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a> served as Kapellmeister to other courts.</p></div>
